  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": "What Happens If My Insurer's Estimate Is Too Low?",
    "text": "What Happens If My Insurer's Estimate Is Too Low?",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "It's super common for an initial insurance estimate to not quite hit the mark, especially with complex interior restoration. Don't sweat it too much if their numbers seem low; it's often just the starting point in the conversation. The key here is good documentation and clear communication. You'll want to make sure your contractor is on the same page with the scope of work and has thoroughly documented all the damage. This means photos, detailed repair plans, and accurate pricing for materials and labor. Sometimes, the initial estimate doesn't account for hidden damage, code upgrades, or the specific materials you need for a like-kind replacement. Your contractor can act as your advocate, providing a detailed, insurance-ready estimate that reflects the true cost of getting your home back to normal. If there's a significant difference, they can communicate directly with the adjuster, explaining why their estimate is more comprehensive. This collaborative approach often leads to a revised, more accurate settlement. Don't be afraid to push back if you believe the scope is incomplete. A pro can really help bridge that gap.",
      "author": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
      }
    }
  },
